1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,904
This is ECE 358, video 22 b. The topic is multiple access protocols, part 2: CSMA/CD and taking turns.

2
00:00:12,354 --> 00:00:32,621
After this video, you can explain CSMA, CSMA/CD and binary exponential backoff. You can calculate backoff times and the CSMA/CD efficiency. And you can explain polling and token passing, and compare the three classes of MAC protocols.

3
00:00:33,071 --> 00:00:43,311
ALOHA transmits without listening. CSMA, carrier sense multiple access, listens first. It senses the channel before it transmits.

4
00:00:43,761 --> 00:00:45,574
Here, A is transmitting.

5
00:00:46,024 --> 00:00:48,456
B gets a frame while A is transmitting.

6
00:00:48,906 --> 00:00:53,749
B senses the channel. It is busy, so B defers its transmission.

7
00:00:54,199 --> 00:00:58,807
When B senses the channel idle, it transmits its entire frame.

8
00:00:59,257 --> 00:01:09,091
CSMA/CD adds collision detection. A node also listens while it transmits. It detects a collision within a short time.

9
00:01:09,541 --> 00:01:14,427
Then it aborts its transmission. This reduces the wasted channel time.

10
00:01:14,877 --> 00:01:19,207
Detection is easy on a wire, but difficult on a wireless link.

11
00:01:19,657 --> 00:01:30,516
Can collisions occur with carrier sensing? Yes. This is a space-time diagram. Distance runs across, with nodes A, B, C and D on one cable.

12
00:01:30,966 --> 00:01:32,481
Time runs down.

13
00:01:32,931 --> 00:01:38,029
At time t zero, B senses the channel idle and starts to transmit.

14
00:01:38,479 --> 00:01:44,986
Its signal spreads to the left and to the right. The slope of the edges shows the propagation delay.

15
00:01:45,436 --> 00:01:52,497
At time t one, D senses the channel. B's first bits have not reached D yet. They arrive later.

16
00:01:52,947 --> 00:01:57,129
So D senses the channel idle, and D also transmits.

17
00:01:57,579 --> 00:02:01,653
Where the two signals overlap, there is a collision. It is red.

18
00:02:02,103 --> 00:02:10,146
Without collision detection, both nodes send their entire frames. So the transmission time of both frames is wasted.

19
00:02:10,596 --> 00:02:23,801
The propagation delay causes this. A larger distance gives a longer time in which a node cannot hear another node's frame. So distance and propagation delay set the collision probability.

20
00:02:24,251 --> 00:02:32,315
Here is the same situation with CSMA/CD. B starts at t zero, and D starts at t one.

21
00:02:32,765 --> 00:02:37,907
D detects the collision when B's first bits arrive at D. D aborts.

22
00:02:38,357 --> 00:02:43,498
B detects the collision when D's first bits arrive at B, and B aborts.

23
00:02:43,948 --> 00:02:54,231
The dashed outlines show the full frames of the last slide, without collision detection. The red area is now much smaller. Much less time is wasted.

24
00:02:54,681 --> 00:03:05,646
A collision at a receiver wastes frame transmissions. To reduce the wasted time, the sender must detect the collision. Then it can abort its transmission early.

25
00:03:06,096 --> 00:03:15,888
Here is the Ethernet CSMA/CD algorithm. Step 1: the NIC gets a datagram from the network layer and creates a frame.

26
00:03:16,338 --> 00:03:26,322
Step 2: the NIC senses the channel. If it is idle, it starts to transmit. If it is busy, it waits until the channel is idle, then transmits.

27
00:03:26,772 --> 00:03:32,831
Step 3: if the NIC sends the entire frame without a collision, it is done with this frame.

28
00:03:33,281 --> 00:03:40,001
Step 4: if the NIC detects another transmission while it sends, it aborts and sends a jam signal.

29
00:03:40,451 --> 00:03:51,459
Step 5: binary exponential backoff. After the m-th collision, the NIC chooses K at random from 0 to 2 to the power m, minus 1.

30
00:03:51,909 --> 00:03:57,093
It waits K times 512 bit times, and goes back to step 2.

31
00:03:57,543 --> 00:04:06,289
After the first collision, m is 1. 2 to the power 1, minus 1, is 1. So K is 0 or 1: 2 choices.

32
00:04:06,739 --> 00:04:14,483
After the second collision, 2 to the power 2, minus 1, is 3. So K is 0 to 3: 4 choices.

33
00:04:14,933 --> 00:04:23,979
After the third collision, K is 0 to 7: 8 choices. After the fourth, K is 0 to 15: 16 choices.

34
00:04:24,429 --> 00:04:32,770
So more collisions give a longer backoff interval. With more choices, two nodes are less likely to choose the same K again.

35
00:04:33,220 --> 00:04:44,185
Slide 35 shows CSMA/CD as a flowchart. An IP packet arrives from the upper layer, and the counter i starts at 0.

36
00:04:44,635 --> 00:04:46,385
The NIC senses the medium.

37
00:04:46,835 --> 00:04:50,013
If it is busy, the NIC waits and senses again.

38
00:04:50,463 --> 00:04:56,991
If the medium is not busy, the NIC transmits the frame, and it detects collisions while it transmits.

39
00:04:57,441 --> 00:05:00,108
With no collision, the result is success.

40
00:05:00,558 --> 00:05:04,334
With a collision, the NIC sends a jamming signal and aborts.

41
00:05:04,784 --> 00:05:09,243
Then it adds 1 to i. So i counts the collisions of this frame.

42
00:05:09,693 --> 00:05:13,554
If i is larger than K max, the result is an error.

43
00:05:14,004 --> 00:05:20,319
Otherwise, the NIC chooses a random number R from 0 to 2 to the power i, minus 1.

44
00:05:20,769 --> 00:05:27,894
It waits R times T P, then senses the medium again. This loop is the exponential backoff.

45
00:05:28,344 --> 00:05:34,765
Slide 36 gives the parameters. The NIC senses the medium for 96 bit times.

46
00:05:35,215 --> 00:05:42,703
The jamming signal is 48 bits. It puts enough energy on the medium so that the others detect the collision.

47
00:05:43,153 --> 00:05:46,119
T P is 512 bit times.

48
00:05:46,569 --> 00:05:56,574
One bit time is 1 over the rate. At 1 Mbps, it is 1 over 10 to the power 6 seconds, which is 1 microsecond.

49
00:05:57,024 --> 00:05:59,499
The counter i stops growing at 10.

50
00:05:59,949 --> 00:06:11,682
Here are the backoff times for a 1 Mbps link. One bit time is 1 over the rate: 1 over 10 to the power 6 seconds, which is 1 microsecond.

51
00:06:12,132 --> 00:06:21,327
So 512 bit times is 512 times 1 microsecond, which is 512 microseconds.

52
00:06:21,777 --> 00:06:29,585
After the first collision, K is 0 or 1. The node waits 0 or 512 microseconds.

53
00:06:30,035 --> 00:06:41,341
After the second collision, K is 0 to 3. The longest wait is 3 times 512, which is 1536 microseconds.

54
00:06:41,791 --> 00:06:53,333
After the third collision, K is 0 to 7. The longest wait is 7 times 512, which is 3584 microseconds.

55
00:06:53,783 --> 00:06:56,449
So the interval doubles after each collision.

56
00:06:56,899 --> 00:07:04,899
At the limit, i equals 10. K can be up to 2 to the power 10, minus 1, which is 1023.

57
00:07:05,349 --> 00:07:18,405
Then the longest wait is 1023 times 512, which is 523 776 microseconds. That is about point 5 2 seconds.

58
00:07:18,855 --> 00:07:30,951
Question 42 asks for the behavior of a CSMA/CD receiver, as a flowchart. Slide 37 gives it in words. Pause the video and try it.

59
00:07:31,401 --> 00:07:38,185
When a frame arrives, the receiver checks if it was received correctly. It verifies the CRC.

60
00:07:38,635 --> 00:07:46,955
If the CRC is valid, the receiver delivers the data in the frame to the protocol above, for example IP.

61
00:07:47,405 --> 00:07:51,096
If the CRC is invalid, it drops the frame.

62
00:07:51,546 --> 00:07:57,242
The receiver sends no ACK. The sender does not learn what the receiver did with the frame.

63
00:07:57,692 --> 00:08:02,471
Recovery of a lost frame is left to upper layers, such as TCP.

64
00:08:02,921 --> 00:08:13,267
Slide 38 gives the efficiency of CSMA/CD. t prop is the maximum propagation delay between two nodes in the LAN.

65
00:08:13,717 --> 00:08:18,368
t trans is the time to transmit a frame of the maximum size.

66
00:08:18,818 --> 00:08:24,173
The efficiency is 1 divided by 1 plus 5 t prop over t trans.

67
00:08:24,623 --> 00:08:30,297
The plot shows the efficiency against the ratio a, which is t prop over t trans.

68
00:08:30,747 --> 00:08:41,414
At a ratio of point 1: 5 times point 1 is point 5. So the efficiency is 1 over 1 point 5, which is point 6 7.

69
00:08:41,864 --> 00:08:49,907
At a ratio of 1: the efficiency is 1 over 1 plus 5, which is 1 over 6, or point 1 7.

70
00:08:50,357 --> 00:08:56,799
The efficiency goes to 1 when t prop goes to 0, or when t trans goes to infinity.

71
00:08:57,249 --> 00:09:05,804
That is, a short cable and long frames give high efficiency. A collision is then found quickly, compared to the frame time.

72
00:09:06,254 --> 00:09:19,225
When t prop is small compared to t trans, CSMA/CD is much better than ALOHA, with point 3 7 or point 1 8. And it is simple, cheap and decentralized.

73
00:09:19,675 --> 00:09:26,928
Compare the classes at low load and at high load. Channel partitioning is efficient and fair at high load.

74
00:09:27,378 --> 00:09:35,250
But at low load, a single active node still gets only 1 over N of the bandwidth, and it must wait for its slot.

75
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:41,460
Random access is the opposite. At low load, one node can use the full channel.

76
00:09:41,910 --> 00:09:44,342
At high load, collisions waste time.

77
00:09:44,792 --> 00:09:53,240
Taking turns protocols look for the best of both: no collisions at high load, and a busy node can use more of the channel.

78
00:09:53,690 --> 00:09:56,954
The slide adds: kind of. They have their own costs.

79
00:09:57,404 --> 00:10:06,748
The first taking turns protocol is polling. It uses a master and slaves. The master invites the other nodes to transmit in turn.

80
00:10:07,198 --> 00:10:11,593
The master polls slave 1: it invites slave 1 to transmit.

81
00:10:12,043 --> 00:10:13,877
Slave 1 sends its data.

82
00:10:14,327 --> 00:10:21,069
Then the master polls slave 2, and slave 2 sends its data. The master continues in turn.

83
00:10:21,519 --> 00:10:29,305
Polling is typically used with simple devices, such as earbuds or a wireless mouse. Bluetooth is an example.

84
00:10:29,755 --> 00:10:34,299
There are three concerns. First, each poll message is overhead.

85
00:10:34,749 --> 00:10:38,888
Second, a node must wait for its poll, which adds latency.

86
00:10:39,338 --> 00:10:45,951
Third, if the master fails, no node can transmit. The master is a single point of failure.

87
00:10:46,401 --> 00:10:55,681
The second taking turns protocol is token passing. A control message, the token, passes from one node to the next in sequence.

88
00:10:56,131 --> 00:11:02,425
Node 1 holds the token, but it has nothing to send. So it passes the token to node 2.

89
00:11:02,875 --> 00:11:11,173
Only the node that holds the token can transmit. Node 2 also has nothing to send, so it passes the token on.

90
00:11:11,623 --> 00:11:17,554
Now node 3 holds the token. It sends its data. Then it passes the token on.

91
00:11:18,004 --> 00:11:25,599
There are three concerns: the token is overhead, nodes wait for the token, and a corrupted token stops the ring.

92
00:11:26,049 --> 00:11:35,201
IBM Token Ring from 1985 used this. It gave a collision-free LAN, but switched Ethernet has replaced it.

93
00:11:35,651 --> 00:11:43,864
Here is the summary of the MAC protocols. Channel partitioning divides the channel by time, frequency or code.

94
00:11:44,314 --> 00:11:51,077
Video 22 a covered time division, TDMA, and frequency division, FDMA.

95
00:11:51,527 --> 00:11:58,460
Random access includes ALOHA, slotted ALOHA, simple CSMA and CSMA/CD.

96
00:11:58,910 --> 00:12:03,283
Carrier sensing is easy on a wire, but hard on wireless links.

97
00:12:03,733 --> 00:12:14,315
Ethernet used CSMA/CD. WiFi, I E E E 802 dot 11, uses CSMA/CA, collision avoidance.

98
00:12:14,765 --> 00:12:23,789
Taking turns includes polling from a central site and token passing. Examples are Bluetooth, F D D I and token ring.

99
00:12:24,239 --> 00:12:32,153
Questions 39 to 41 are about CSMA and CSMA/CD. Pause the video and try it.

100
00:12:32,603 --> 00:12:44,294
Question 39. In simple CSMA, a node senses the channel before it transmits. If idle, it sends the entire frame. If busy, it defers.

101
00:12:44,744 --> 00:12:57,651
Question 40. Draw the flowchart from slide 35: sense, transmit while detecting collisions, and on a collision send a jam signal, abort, and back off for a random time.

102
00:12:58,101 --> 00:13:12,479
Question 41. The cause is propagation delay. Another node may have started already, but its bits had not reached this node when it sensed. In the diagram, D senses idle after B has started.

103
00:13:12,929 --> 00:13:20,524
Question 43 asks for the purpose of each feature of CSMA/CD. Pause the video and try it.

104
00:13:20,974 --> 00:13:27,630
Carrier sensing: a node does not start while another node transmits. This reduces collisions.

105
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:33,136
Wait: when the channel is busy, the node defers until the channel is idle.

106
00:13:33,586 --> 00:13:41,010
Collision detection while transmitting: the sender finds a collision quickly, so it can abort and waste less time.

107
00:13:41,460 --> 00:13:48,756
Jamming bits: 48 bits put enough energy on the medium so that all the other nodes detect the collision.

108
00:13:49,206 --> 00:13:59,339
Exponential backoff: each node waits a random time, so the colliding nodes choose different times. After more collisions, the interval is longer.

109
00:13:59,789 --> 00:14:06,211
Question 44 has six parts about taking turns protocols. Pause the video and try it.

110
00:14:06,661 --> 00:14:13,615
Part a. The nodes take turns to use the channel, and nodes with more to send can take longer turns.

111
00:14:14,065 --> 00:14:22,620
Part b. In polling, the master gives turns to the slaves. In token passing, the node that holds the token has the turn.

112
00:14:23,070 --> 00:14:28,979
Part c. The node transmits its data. With nothing to send, it passes the turn on.

113
00:14:29,429 --> 00:14:33,568
Part d. The two strategies are polling and token passing.

114
00:14:34,018 --> 00:14:44,023
Part e. Polling has a central master that invites each node. Token passing has no master. A control token passes from node to node.

115
00:14:44,473 --> 00:14:52,196
Part f. The drawbacks are overhead, latency, and a single point of failure: the master, or a corrupted token.

116
00:14:52,646 --> 00:15:00,134
To recap. CSMA senses the channel first, but collisions still occur because of propagation delay.

117
00:15:00,584 --> 00:15:11,507
CSMA/CD also aborts on a collision, sends a jam signal and backs off for a random time. The interval doubles after each collision.

118
00:15:11,957 --> 00:15:21,962
Its efficiency is high when the propagation delay is small compared to the frame time. The receiver checks the CRC and sends no ACK.

119
00:15:22,412 --> 00:15:29,367
Taking turns uses polling or a token. The next video covers MAC addresses, ARP and Ethernet.
